'''Darline Lettie Graham Nordone'''<ref>{{Cite web |title=Sen. Darline Graham - R South Carolina, In Office - Biography |website=[[LegiStorm]] |url=https://www.legistorm.com/person/bio/597767/Darline_Lettie_Graham_Nordone.html |access-date=July 29, 2026 |language=en}}</ref> (born '''Darlene Lettie Graham''';{{efn|{{IPAc-en|g|r|æ|m}} {{respell|GRAM}}}} June 12, 1964) is an American politician and [[Public administration|public administrator]] serving as the [[Seniority in the United States Senate|junior]] [[United States senator]] from [[South Carolina]], a seat she has held since July 2026. A member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], she was appointed on the recommendation of President [[Donald Trump]] to fill the vacancy created by the death of her older brother, [[Lindsey Graham]]. She is the first sister to succeed her brother in office in the history of the United States Senate. She is also the [[List of the first women holders of political offices in the United States|first woman]] to represent South Carolina in the United States Senate.

Graham was the Republican nominee in the [[2026 United States Senate election in South Carolina]], losing to [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] nominee [[Annie Andrews]] in an [[Upset (competition)|upset]].

== Early life and education ==
[[File:Darline Graham Nordone butter cow.jpg|thumb|300px|Darline Graham with her brother Lindsey at the 2015 [[Iowa State Fair]]]]
Darlene<!-- Her name at birth was spelled Darlene; do not change to Darline --> Lettie Graham<ref name="The Atlantic Tea Party">{{Cite web |last=Ball |first=Molly |author-link=Molly Ball |date=June 10, 2014 |title=How Lindsey Graham Stomped the Tea Party |url=https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2014/06/how-lindsey-graham-stomped-the-tea-party/372521/ |url-access=subscription |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260410183321/https://www.theatlantic.com/politics/archive/2014/06/how-lindsey-graham-stomped-the-tea-party/372521/ |archive-date=April 10, 2026 |access-date=July 13, 2026 |work=[[The Atlantic]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://archive.org/details/columns1983colu |title=Columns, 1983 |publisher=Anderson College |date=1983 |access-date=October 31, 2026}}</ref> was born in [[Central, South Carolina]], on June 12, 1964.<ref>{{CongBio |id=G000608 |name=GRAHAM, Darline |date=July 14, 2026|inline=yes}}</ref><ref name="NYTimes Bond">{{Cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/13/us/lindsey-graham-sister-darline-nordone.html |title=From a Young Age, Graham and His Sister Had a Tight Bond |date=July 13, 2026 |last=Baker |first=Mike |newspaper=[[The New York Times]] |access-date=July 13, 2026 |url-access=subscription |id={{ProQuest|3363166393}}}}</ref><ref name="DavenportAdcox2008-1">{{Cite web |url=http://www.beaufortgazette.com/local/story/454820.html |title=South Carolina Senate GOP primary is 'David and Goliath' |date=June 2, 2008 |last1=Davenport |first1=Jim |last2=Adcox |first2=Seanna |publisher=[[Associated Press]] |work=[[The Beaufort Gazette]] |access-date=July 13, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080606035357/http://www.beaufortgazette.com/local/story/454820.html |archive-date=June 6, 2008 |url-status=dead}}</ref> Her parents, Florence James "F. J." Graham and Millie Graham (née Walters),<ref name="Summers2015-1" /><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/24250356/millie-walters-graham-1976-obituary/ |title=Mrs. Florence Graham |work=The Greenville News |date=June 11, 1976 |access-date=October 28, 2026}}</ref> operated a restaurant, bar, liquor store, and pool hall, and the family lived in rooms behind the business.<ref name="NYTimes Bond" /><ref name="DavenportAdcox2008-1" /> Her mother died of cancer when Graham was 11, and her father died of a heart attack 15 months later.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Binday |first=Ben |date=July 13, 2026 |title=Darline Graham Nordone has little political history beyond praising her brother |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/07/13/darline-graham-nordone-has-little-political-history-beyond-praising-her-brother/ |access-date=July 14, 2026 |newspaper=The Washington Post |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286 |url-access=subscription}}</ref><ref name="Summers2015-1">{{cite web |last1=Summers |first1=Juana |title=5 things you should know about Lindsey Graham |url=https://www.npr.org/sections/itsallpolitics/2015/05/31/410358726/5-things-you-should-know-about-lindsey-graham |publisher=[[NPR]] |access-date=July 14, 2026 |date=May 31, 2015 |archive-date=July 4, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170704143440/http://www.npr.org/sections/itsallpolitics/2015/05/31/410358726/5-things-you-should-know-about-lindsey-graham |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="ShabadCoronell_Uribe2026-1" /> After their deaths, Graham's brother [[Lindsey Graham|Lindsey]] arranged for her to live with an aunt and uncle in [[Seneca, South Carolina]]. Before joining the [[United States Air Force|Air Force]] in 1982, Lindsey, nine years her senior, became Graham's [[legal guardian]].<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Blair |first=Caroline |date=July 13, 2026 |title=Who Is Lindsey Graham's Sister? What to Know About Darline Graham Nordone |url=https://people.com/all-about-lindsey-graham-sister-darline-graham-12017033 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260713191656/https://people.com/all-about-lindsey-graham-sister-darline-graham-12017033 |archive-date=July 13, 2026 |access-date=July 13, 2026 |magazine=[[People (magazine)|People]] |language=en |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="The State Ads">{{Cite news |url=https://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/politics-columns-blogs/the-buzz/article13855640.html |title=Lindsey Graham's sister discusses childhood, losing parents in senator's new campaign ads |date=May 20, 2014 |last=Self |first=Jamie |newspaper=[[The State (newspaper)|The State]] |access-date=July 13, 2026 |archive-date=October 1, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201001080634/https://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/politics-columns-blogs/the-buzz/article13855640.html |url-status=live}}</ref> He taught her to spell her first name "Darline", a spelling she continues to use in adulthood.<ref name="The Atlantic Tea Party"/>

Graham graduated from the [[College of Charleston]] with a Bachelor of Science in sociology in 1989.<ref name="Post and Courier 2026-07-13">{{Cite news |first=Caitlin |last=Byrd |date=July 13, 2026 |title=Who is Darline Graham? Lindsey Graham's sister and South Carolina's interim US senator. |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/politics/lindsey-darline-graham-nordone-senator-sc/article_68b21a19-ab4e-4f0d-9542-46277a343487.html |newspaper=[[The Post and Courier]] |access-date=July 14, 2026}}</ref> She earned a Master of Arts in [[rehabilitation counseling]] from [[South Carolina State University]] in 2009,<ref>{{cite web |last1=Santaella |first1=Tony |title=Who is Darline Graham? Meet the woman appointed to temporarily fill Lindsey Graham's U.S. Senate seat |url=https://www.wltx.com/article/news/local/darline-graham-nordone-new-south-carolina-senator/101-5ca37bf5-4cff-408c-8f93-159faba814b7 |publisher=[[WLTX]] |access-date=July 13, 2026 |date=July 13, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Darline Graham, Senator for South Carolina |publisher=[[United States Senate]] |url=https://www.dgraham.senate.gov/ |access-date=July 14, 2026 |archive-date=July 14, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260714190540/https://www.dgraham.senate.gov/ |url-status=live}}</ref> and is a [[certified public manager]].<ref>{{cite web |url= https://governor.sc.gov/news/2026-07/gov-mcmaster-appoints-darline-graham-us-senate |title= Gov. McMaster Appoints Darline Graham to the U.S. Senate |publisher= Office of the Governor of South Carolina |date= July 13, 2026 |access-date=October 13, 2026}}</ref>

== Career ==
=== Public administration ===
In 2007, Graham began working for the South Carolina Vocational Rehabilitation Department.<ref name="Reid2008-1" /><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.darlinegraham.com/ |title=Darline Graham for U.S. Senate, home page |publisher=Darline Graham's campaign |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20261029021125/https://www.darlinegraham.com/ |archive-date=October 29, 2026 |access-date=October 28, 2026}}</ref> She helped coordinate a [[remote work]] program for adults with severe disabilities for [[Time Warner]].<ref name="Reid2008-1">{{Cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/article/the-state-new-program-offers-another-ch/201549634/ |title=New program offers 'another chance at life' |date=July 18, 2008 |last=Reid |first=Czerne |newspaper=[[The State (newspaper)|The State]] |page=32 |access-date=July 13, 2026 |via=[[Newspapers.com]]}}</ref> She was the department's public information director in 2015.<ref>{{Cite news |date=June 2, 2015 |title=Austin Industrial Honored |url=https://newspaperarchive.com/us/south-carolina/aiken/aiken-standard/2015/06-02/page-9 |url-access=subscription |access-date=July 15, 2026 |work=[[Aiken Standard]] |pages=9 |via=[[NewspaperArchive]]}}</ref>

In 2019, Graham became commissioner and agency head of the South Carolina Commission for the Blind, which administers [[vocational rehabilitation]], [[independent living]], and blindness-prevention programs.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Luterman |first=Sara |date=July 17, 2026 |title=Darline Graham's career centered disability. Now she could help shape national policy. |url=https://19thnews.org/2026/07/darline-graham-disability-career-senate/ |access-date=July 18, 2026 |website=The 19th |language=en-us |archive-date=July 17, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260717213821/https://19thnews.org/2026/07/darline-graham-disability-career-senate/ |url-status=live}}</ref> She serves on the South Carolina State Workforce Development Board and its works management committee.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.scworks.org/state-workforce-development-board-members |title=State Workforce Development Board Members |publisher=South Carolina State Workforce Development Board |access-date=October 28, 2026}}</ref> In 2026, Graham became president-elect of the National Council of State Agencies for the Blind. Her term is scheduled to begin in January 2027.<ref name="Post and Courier 2026-07-13" />

== Political career ==

=== Lindsey Graham campaigns ===
In 2002, Graham appeared in a television advertisement for her brother's campaign in the [[2002 United States Senate election in South Carolina|United States Senate election in South Carolina]].<ref name="The State 2002-10-14"/> She assisted him in subsequent campaigns, and discussed her childhood during the [[2014 United States Senate election in South Carolina|2014]]<ref name="The State Ads" /> and [[2026 United States Senate election in South Carolina|2026]] Senate races.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-07-13/trump-recommends-graham-s-sister-to-serve-out-his-senate-term |title=Trump Recommends Graham's Sister Serve Out His Senate Term |date=July 13, 2026 |last1=Lowenkron |first1=Hadriana |last2=Wasson |first2=Erik |publisher=[[Bloomberg News]] |access-date=July 13, 2026}}</ref> Graham supported her brother's [[Lindsey Graham 2016 presidential campaign|campaign]] for [[president of the United States]] in the [[2016 United States presidential election|2016 election]], introducing him in his first campaign speech.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/aikenstandard/news/sen-lindsey-graham-i-want-to-be-president/article_a453afc6-8183-5e4d-bc8b-647ecf5283ee.html |title=Sen. Lindsey Graham: 'I want to be president' |date=June 1, 2015 |last=Asberry |first=Derrek |work=[[Aiken Standard]] |access-date=July 13, 2026 |url-access=subscription}}</ref>

=== U.S. Senate (2026–present) ===
[[File:Senator Chuck Grassley Swears In Darline Graham As Senator Of South Carolina.jpg|right|thumb|Graham was ceremonially sworn in by [[Chuck Grassley]] on July 14, 2026]]

==== Appointment ====
On July 13, 2026, South Carolina governor [[Henry McMaster]] appointed Graham to fill her brother's [[Legislative vacancy (United States)|vacant]] Senate seat.<ref name= "ShabadCoronell_Uribe2026-1">{{Cite web |date= July 13, 2026 |title= South Carolina governor appoints Lindsey Graham's sister to finish his Senate term |url= https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/trump-lindsey-graham-sister-darline-mcmaster-appoint-senate-term-rcna587302 |access-date= July 13, 2026 |publisher= NBC News |language= en |first1= Rebecca |last1= Shabad |first2= Raquel |last2= Coronell Uribe}}</ref> President [[Donald Trump]] endorsed her the same day.<ref>{{Cite news |url= https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/07/13/trump-recommends-lindsey-grahams-sister-serve-rest-his-senate-term/ |title= Trump recommends that Lindsey Graham's sister serve rest of his Senate term |date= July 13, 2026 |last1= Koseff |first1= Alexei |last2= Zakrzewski |first2= Cat |newspaper= [[The Washington Post]] |access-date= July 13, 2026 |archive-date= July 13, 2026 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260713160558/https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/07/13/trump-recommends-lindsey-grahams-sister-serve-rest-his-senate-term/ |url-status= live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url= https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/24/us/politics/trump-lindsey-graham-sister-darline-senate.html |title= How a Grieving Trump Decided to Back Lindsey Graham's Sister for Senate |last= Davies |first= Emily |work= The New York Times |date= July 24, 2026 |access-date= October 28, 2026}}</ref> Graham was sworn in on July 14, becoming the first woman to represent South Carolina in the Senate<ref>{{Cite web |last= Hubbard |first= Kaia |title= Darline Graham, Lindsey Graham's sister, sworn in to serve the remainder of Senate term |url= https://www.cbsnews.com/news/darline-graham-sworn-in-senate-lindsey-graham-sister/ |access-date= July 14, 2026 |publisher= [[CBS News]] |language= en |date= July 14, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date= July 14, 2026 |title= Darline Graham, sister of Lindsey Graham, sworn in to fulfill his Senate term |url= https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/jul/14/darline-graham-nordone-south-carolina-senator |access-date= July 14, 2026 |website= The Guardian |language= en |first1= Shrai |last1= Popat}}</ref> and the first sister to replace a sibling in the Senate.<ref name= Eskow>{{Cite web |url= https://rollcall.com/2026/07/14/darline-graham-sworn-in-senate-sister/ |title= Darline Graham sworn in to the Senate as first sister to succeed a brother |first= Nick |last= Eskow |date= July 14, 2026 |website= Roll Call |access-date= July 16, 2026 |archive-date= July 16, 2026 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20260716152314/https://rollcall.com/2026/07/14/darline-graham-sworn-in-senate-sister/ |url-status= live| quote= While a number of widows have succeeded their late husbands in Congress, and brothers have previously succeeded siblings, Graham is the first sister to do so in U.S. history. She is also the first sibling to directly succeed a deceased senator and the first woman to serve in the Senate from South Carolina.}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url= https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/25/us/politics/darline-lindsey-graham-election-succession.html |title= Would Darline Graham Be the First Sister to Succeed a Brother in Congress? |last= Liederman |first= Mack |work= The New York Times |date= August 25, 2026 |access-date= October 28, 2026}}</ref>

==== 2026 election and debates ====
{{Main|2026 United States Senate election in South Carolina}}
President Trump publicly encouraged Graham on July 17 to run in the [[2026 United States Senate election in South Carolina]] and promised to endorse her if she did.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Johansen |first1=Ben |title=Trump endorses Darline Graham for Lindsay Graham's open Senate seat |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2026/07/17/trump-endorses-darline-graham-for-lindsay-grahams-open-senate-seat-01003457 |work=[[Politico]] |access-date=July 18, 2026 |date=July 17, 2026}}</ref> Graham told [[Sean Hannity]] three days later that she intended to run for a full term.<ref>{{Cite news |first=Kaia |last=Hubbard |date=July 20, 2026 |title=Darline Graham, Lindsey Graham's sister, says she'll run for full Senate term |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/darline-graham-lindsey-graham-sister-senate-full-term-campaign/ |access-date=July 20, 2026 |publisher=CBS News |language=en-US}}</ref> Graham came in first in the special primary election on August 11, advancing to a runoff with [[Ralph Norman]].<ref>{{Cite news |first=Julia |last=Mueller |date=August 11, 2026 |title=Graham, Norman advance to runoff in South Carolina GOP primary for Senate |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/6022968-graham-norman-south-carolina-gop-senate-primary-runoff/ |access-date=August 11, 2026 |work=The Hill |language=en-US}}</ref> On August 25, Graham won the runoff, advancing to the general election against Democratic nominee [[Annie Andrews]],<ref>{{Cite news |last1= Medina |first1= Eduardo |last2= Wagner |first2= Bayliss |last3= Davies |first3= Emily |last4= Mazzei |first4= Patricia |date= August 25, 2026 |title= South Carolina U.S. Senate Special Runoff 2026: Live Election Results, Graham vs. Norman |url= https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2026/08/25/us/elections/results-south-carolina-us-senate-special-runoff-august-25.html |access-date= August 26, 2026 |work= The New York Times |language= en-US |issn= 0362-4331}}</ref> [[Constitution Party (United States)|Constitution Party]] nominee Mark Hackett, [[Libertarian Party (United States)|Libertarian Party]] nominee Kasie Whitener, and write-in candidate [[Catherine Fleming Bruce]]. 

Most polls found Graham to be within the [[margin of error]] or slightly behind Andrews, but she remained a moderate favorite due to South Carolina's partisan lean and tradition of [[straight-ticket voting]].<ref name= "Dawsey2026-1">{{Cite news |last= Dawsey |first= Josh |date= 2026-11-01 |title= Inside the Final Days of Darline Graham's Reluctant Senate Run |url= https://www.wsj.com/politics/elections/darline-graham-senate-race-da6227b0 |access-date= 2026-11-01 |work= Wall Street Journal |language= en-US |issn= 0099-9660}}</ref><ref name= "Davies2026-1">{{Cite news |last= Davies |first= Emily |date= 2026-10-30 |title= Darline Graham Is 'a Little Nervous.' Can She Keep South Carolina's Senate Seat Red? |url= https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/30/us/darline-graham-annie-andrews-south-carolina-senate.html |access-date= 2026-11-01 |work= The New York Times |language= en-US |issn= 0362-4331}}</ref> On November 3, Andrews won the election in an [[Upset (competition)|upset]].<ref name= "andrews_wins">{{cite web |last1= Hubbard |first1= Kaia |title= Annie Andrews wins South Carolina 2026 Senate election, flipping seat for Democrats |url= https://www.cbsnews.com/news/south-carolina-2026-senate-annie-andrews-darline-graham/ |website= [[CBS News]] |access-date= 2026-11-03 |date=2026-11-03}}</ref>

Graham's gaffe during a primary runoff debate that "[[national security]] is not my thing,” was seen as a catalyst to her defeat, as some voters began to see her as inexperienced and unqualified, inflaming a divide between establishment and conservative Republicans<ref>{{Cite news |date=November 3, 2026 |title=Congressman Ralph Norman reacts to Annie Andrews beating Darline Graham for SC Senate seat |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GLkuf5sCRmk |access-date=November 6, 2026 |work=Queen City News}}</ref> who objected to Trump's involvement in the race.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Gallagher |first=Dianne |date=November 4, 2026 |title=Trump's personal push of Darline Graham in South Carolina comes back to bite his party |url= https://www.cnn.com/2026/11/03/politics/trump-darline-graham-loss |access-date=November 8, 2026 |work= CNN |language=en}}</ref>

==== Tenure ====
[[File:P20260918JB-1120 (55542038343).jpg|thumb|Graham and Trump in the Oval Office after signing the [[Sanctioning Russia Act]], September 18, 2026]]
On July 16, Graham introduced the [[Lindsey O. Graham Sanctioning Russia and Iran Act of 2026]], legislation to impose sanctions on Russia. It is based on the [[Sanctioning Russia Act]], a bill Lindsey Graham authored and supported, and had bipartisan support.<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 16, 2026 |title=Risch, Graham, Blumenthal Lead 60+ Senators in Announcing Agreement on Legislation to Hold Purchasers of Russian Oil Accountable |publisher=United States Senate Committee on Foreign Relations |url=https://www.foreign.senate.gov/press/rep/release/risch-graham-blumenthal-lead-60-senators-in-announcing-agreement-on-legislation-to-hold-purchasers-of-russian-oil-accountable |access-date=July 18, 2026 |language=en}}</ref> It passed the Senate in August<ref>{{Cite web |title=Senate passes sanctions bill on Russia and Iran named for late Sen. Lindsey Graham |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/congress/senate-passes-sanctions-bill-russia-iran-named-late-sen-lindsey-graham-rcna589949|access-date=September 29, 2026 |publisher=NBC News |language=en-US|date=August 7, 2026|first1=Sahil|last1=Kapur|first2=Frank |last2=Thorpe V}}</ref> and passed in the House in mid-September with bipartisan support. President Trump signed it into law two days later.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Trump signs bill imposing sanctions on Russia and giving him more power to levy tariffs |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/sep/18/trump-signs-russia-sanctions-bill|access-date=September 29, 2026 |publisher=The Guardian |language=en-US|date=September 18, 2026|first1=Robert|last1=Tait}}</ref>

Graham voted to confirm [[Todd Blanche]] as [[United States Attorney General]].<ref>{{Cite web |title= Sen. Darline Graham votes to confirm Todd Blanche as attorney general |url= https://wpde.com/news/local/sen-darline-graham-votes-to-confirm-todd-blanche-as-attorney-general-50-49-vote-r-south-carolina-us| access-date= September 30, 2026 |publisher= WPDE |language= en-US| date= September 18, 2026| first1= Elizabeth| last1= Foley}}</ref>

